 "_note": "Pest, disease and farming terms in the machine-translated languages, written by hand because the translators read them as everyday words: 'Rice blast' came back as a rice explosion, 'Aphids' in Malayalam as rabbits, 'Rice hispa' as papaya, pumpkin, gram flour and swan, 'False smut' as a lie, 'Wilt' as silence and as mischief, 'Ear rot' as rot of a person's ear. Where the state agricultural university's extension leaflets have a settled local name it is used (TNAU, ANGRAU, UAS, KAU, AAU/GAU, PAU, BCKV); where not, the English name is written in the script with a plain word for what it is (disease, beetle, caterpillar), so it can never mean something else. 'Rice' is the crop (paddy), not the grain. 'terms' are put into every sentence that names them; 'names_only' are too common as plain words to swap inside a sentence ('wilt' is also a verb, 'brown spot' also a description) and are used where the text is the name itself. Field words are here too, after a sentence-by-sentence check found '10 hills' (rice clumps) translated as ten mountains, 'at heading' as 'in the title', 'dead hearts' as dead human hearts and 'cowpea' as cow-dung pulse. Pending review by a native speaker.",
